{"nebelwerfer41_2" "The 150mm Nebelwerfer 41 was a German multiple rocket launcher used in the Second World War for saturating fire. Its main drawbacks included heavy smoke generated by the launchers which meant that it had to be relocated quickly due to counter-artillery."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "sdkfz250_1" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:137), not in any buy list
{"sdkfz250_1" "The SdKfz 250/1 Was a light armoured halftrack, very similar in appearance to the larger Hanomag-designed SdKfz 251, and built by the DEMAG firm, for use by Nazi Germany in World War II. Most variants were open-topped and had a single access door in the rear mount with a machine gun."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "mg34_stan" without a buy button (units/vehicles_xnotanks.set:26), not in any buy list
{"mg34_stan" "The Maschinengewehr 34, or MG 34, is a German recoil-operated air-cooled machine gun, first tested in 1929, introduced in 1934, and issued to units in 1936. It introduced an entirely new concept in automatic firepower, the Einheitsmaschinengewehr Universal machine gun and is generally considered the world's first general purpose machine gun."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "t34_3_ger2" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:89), not in any buy list
{"t34_3_ger2" "This modification entered service with the Red Army on January 23rd, 1944. In order to mount a powerful 85mm anti-tank gun, some changes had to be introduced into its turret design: it became larger and more heavily protected. Its side and rear armor was also enhanced, which, in combination with its sharp slope angle, significantly increased the shell resistance of the hull. As a result the T-34's weight increased to 32 tons, making the tank a bit slower, but its combat efficiency rose significantly."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "sgrw42_ger2" without a buy button (units/vehicles_xnotanks.set:33), not in any buy list
{"sgrw42_ger2" "The design of this 81mm gun closely follows its Soviet counterpart, consisting of three main sections: the firing tube, support plate and a two-legged mount. It fires 3.5kg HE rounds via indirect trajectory. Firing is triggered by the pin inside the tube striking the primer of a descending shell."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "sfh18_ger2" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:221), not in any buy list
{"sfh18_ger2" "In production since 1918, the SFH-18 heavy field howitzer was used by the Wehrmacht troops during WWII, against enemy artillery and personnel, as well as fortifications and trench lines. It fired 43kg HE rounds, capable of dealing devastating damage at very long distances, but the gun's heavy weight impaired its mobility."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key
{"pz4h_ger2" "In April 1943, another modification of the most popular German tank came to the front. This machine differed from its predecessors, having enhanced armor and improved armament. Its armor was 80mm thick, and the mounted screens on its sides ensured reliable protection from high-explosion rounds. The new long-barrelled Kwk40 L/48 boasted excellent combat performance and was successfully used to fight medium enemy tanks."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key
{"pz4g_ger2" "The first version of the Pz.IV assault tank turned out to be ineffective against Soviet T34s and KVs. However, by the spring of 1942, the Wehrmacht started receiving new versions of the PzKpfw IV tanks, complete with upgraded armament and long-barrelled anti-tank Kwk40 guns, capable of defeating almost any opponent. In addition, the tank had good manoeuvrability, which -- along with its strong armor and powerful gun -- turned it into a fearsome combat vehicle."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key
{"pz3m_ger2" "In order to clear minefields, German engineers developed a special mine roller. When mounted upon the chassis of a Pz.Kpfw.III tank, it allowed the vehicle to drive through minefields unharmed. The resulting corridors were then used by infantry and ordinary tanks. It was armed with only a machine-gun."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key
{"pz3f_ger2" "The PzKpfw III Ausf. F was intended to fight alongside other tanks, using the same 37mm PaK 36 type gun favoured by the infantry at that time. This cannon provided adequate armament until 1940. Thankfully, the Panzer III design accommodated a larger armament, greatly increasing its lifespan once a larger cannon was required."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "jagdtiger_ger2" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:93), not in any buy list
{"jagdtiger_ger2" "In the summer of 1944, the Wehrmacht received their heaviest tank destroyer ever -- the Jagdtiger. Built on the King Tiger chassis, its main drawback was its exceptional weight, which gave it low mobility. However, in terms of armor and firepower, this vehicle was unmatched -- its 128mm long-barrelled gun was able to destroy any adversary with ease, and the thickness of its armor maxed out at as much as 250mm."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key
{"flak38_wheeled_ger2" "Mounted on a wheeled carriage, the FlaK 38 gun was a further development of the FlaK-30 AA gun: lighter, but boasting higher rate of fire. It was used by the Wehrmacht and Luftwaffe for anti-aircraft defense. It could, however, be easily employed against infantry and lightly-armored vehicles."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "flak37_ger2" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:219), not in any buy list
{"flak37_ger2" "The 88mm FlaK 18 was an anti-aircraft and anti-tank gun, mounted on a gun carriage that enabled it to fire in all directions. Widespread production started in 1933, and it soon proved to be the best anti-aircraft weapon available, when Germany intervened during the Spanish Civil War. It also turned out to be an excellent anti-tank weapon, as its high muzzle velocity and large calibre gave it the power and range to take out enemy armor. The success of this weapon lead to many variants, and it was one of the most recognizable German weapons throughout the war."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "m114_vet" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:193), not in any buy list
{"m114_vet" "This heavy field howitzer design was based on the old 155mm French guns and entered into mass production in 1941. Intended for indirect fire on enemy personnel, fortifications and important shallow objectives, the M114 proved to be an excellent tool on the battlefield, and its easy maintenance, good ballistics and sturdy construction made it popular among US artillery troops."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "longtom_155mm_vet" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:194), not in any buy list
{"longtom_155mm_vet" "Before entering World War I, the United States was poorly equipped with heavy artillery. To address this problem a number of foreign heavy artillery guns were adopted, including the Canon de 155 mm GPF. After the end of the war development work began in the United States on a design to improve upon the existing models of heavy gun and carriage. A number of prototypes were produced in the 1920s and 1930s, but the projects were put on hold due to lack of funds. In 1938 the 155 mm Gun T4 on Carriage T2 was finally adopted as 155mm gun M1 on Carriage M1."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "m115_vet" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:195), not in any buy list
{"m115_vet" "The M115 203 mm howitzer, also known as the M115 8 inch howitzer was a towed howitzer developed and used by the United States Army."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key
{"np_t48_us" "T48 57mm GMC - M3 based Gun Motor Carriage equipped with the M1 57mm gun, an American copy of the British QF 6 pounder anti-tank gun. A total of 962 T48s were produced during the war. Of these, 60 were supplied under lend lease to Britain, and 650 to the USSR - who called it SU-57 (99 rounds)"}
; [changed] no buy-list card names this key
{"m19" "This anachronism of a self-propelled anti-aircraft gun was developed in 1945 and was primarily used for AA defense of vehicles on the move and in deployment sites. The M24 \"Chaffee\" chassis provided good speed and manoeuvrability, and two paired 40mm Bofors guns were able to engage not only planes, but also light and even medium enemy tanks. The first M19s were ready by the end of war, but never actually saw action. This is a World War II game you morons..."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key
{"m4a1_76w" "M4A1 76mm gun. Later M4A1 model received the larger T23 turret with a high-velocity 76mm M1 gun, which reduced the number of HE and smoke rounds carried and increased the number of anti-tank rounds. The first standard-production 76 mm gun Sherman was an M4A1, accepted in January 1944."}
; [new] no buy-list card names this key | only on card "wasp" without a buy button (units/vehicles_x.set:61), not in any buy list
{"wasp" "A Universal Carrier with a Ronson flamethrower fitted. This vehicle is a perfect asset when you have too much of the enemy existing in one place and you want to change that situation."}
